* trans studies and the materialization of gender
** abstract
This paper brings theorizing from Trans Studies to bear on current
methods for detecting and mitigating anti-trans bias in LLM-generated
text. It looks at common frameworks of evaluating gender and
anti-trans bias in LLMs and weighs these in light of recent theorizing
in Trans Studies. In particular, Kadji Amin’s framework of gender
identification, which promotes a definition for gender categories
based on visible and expressive qualities. The survey of current tools
is followed by a discussion on implications of marking gender as
identity or as expression. It then reviews some recent work in
evaluating gender bias that makes some progress in this area, and
makes suggestions for future research.

** introduction
There is a global urgency to conceptualize gender as a category. In
various countries around the world, the emergence and increasingly
visible use of gender terms and identities like transgender and
nonbinary have prompted a backlash that has resulted in the retraction
of hard-gained rights for LGBTQ+ groups, such as in the USA most
recently, with the ban on gender-affirming care for minors (Karni and
Kavi, 2025). This backlash takes what it calls "gender ideology", or
nontraditional views of gender, like those that are not based on
biological sex, as a rallying cry for a host of right-wing political
priorities. Presenting gender ideology in what Judith Butler describes
as a "phantasm", discrimination against trans and nonbinary groups has
been linked to other kinds of social discrimination, such as
xenophobic and anti-immigrant perspectives (Human Rights Research
Center, 2025).

In response to attacks on what it calls "gender ideology," some
progressive groups have doubled down on a definition of gender that is
based on internal identification rather than biological reality
(Dresher 2025). Drawing from seminal theories of gender, such as
Butler's theory of Gender Performativity (1990, discussed in more
detail below), this perspective of gender leaves room for individuals
to identify in ways that may or may not correspond to their assigned
sex at birth.

This paper explores how these global debates about gender as a
category emerge in recent NLP methods for studying transgender bias in
language. Language forms, which implicitly encode perceptions on
gender into concrete linguistic features, NLP attempts to make
explicit through various methods for studying and mitigating this bias
in language. Methods have ranged from Word Embedding approaches
(Bolukbasi et al. 2016), to Coreference Resolution tasks (Rudinger et
al. 2018), and most recently, Language Generation methods (Kaneko et
al. 2024). 

This paper explores how these such methods might gain from considering
definitions of gender from the field of Trans Studies. Specifically,
it considers current conversations in Trans Studies that push against
dominant conceptualizations of gender today, on both the conservative
and progressive side. This perspective, also called "trans
materialism", is based on the notion that gender ought to be conceived
as a social and behavioral phenomenon, instead of an internal identity
(Amin 2022). Its logic states that if gender is conceived as an
outward phenomenon, then it will be easier to make visible those who
are non-conforming, and therefore most marginalized, by gender norms.

It then explores how these conceptualizations might be applied to
current methods in NLP, focusing on two recent methods for detecting
transgender bias in language. It examines these two methods, TIDEs and
EQUITABL (Lameiro et al. 2025, Devinney et al. 2024) in depth, in a
close examination of methodology, to draw out the potential sites of
engagement with Trans Studies theory. Despite their constrasting
approaches from these methods, one focusing on binary classification
and the other on qualitative analysis, both approaches significantly
gain from a consideration of Trans Studies theorizing on gender bias.
This theorization, emphasizes the importance of conceptualizing gender
as a visible marker, has direct influence on the labels and contexts
given to gender in NLP.

A centeral part of this paper's argument, which it demonstrates in
practice through its deep and close analysis of NLP methodology, is
that individualized and sustained attention to the object of study,
what in Humanist fields is called "close reading," helps to
materialize and concretize its workings. This paper therefore
contributes to work that calls for and brings interdisciplinary
theorizing into NLP, specifically the context of this conference (Wang
et al. 2025, Birhane et al. 2022). Bringing Trans Studies to bear on
NLP opens a perspective that is little known outside of the field,
but which can offer guidance and direction for NLP efforts to study
and reduce gender bias in language.

** Gender ideology
The term "gender ideology," which the Southern Poverty Law Center
classifies as derogatory, refers to the conservative and far-right
view that LGBTQ+ identity is an internal and false belief system (SPLC
2023).

In various countries around the world, gender ideology has become a
national issue, serving as a rallying cry for various political groups
to garner support. For example, in the European continent, far-right
groups have used transphobia to prop up other fears, such as those
against migrants, in what (Indelicato and Lopes, 2024) argue is part
of a larger white supremacist campaign. In the African continent, the
same fear has been deployed in anti-colonial discourses, which assert
that gender ideology is a dangerous import from the West which
threatens traditional gender roles and the nuclear family. The past
few years have seen a wave of anti-LGBT+ legislation, for example the
law in Uganda that penalizes "aggravated homosexuality" with the death
penalty (BBC 2023), and in the USA, since 2020, each year the number
of anti-trans bills being proposed surpasses the last, with 2025
proposing over 1000 such bills in legislative houses across the
country (Trans Legislation Tracker 2025). Additionally, in the USA, a
mounting wave of transphobia has propelled a series of Executive
Orders, including "Defending Women From Gender Ideology Extremism And
Restoring Biological Truth To The Federal Government" and "Keeping Men
Out of Women's Sports". These initiatives double down on the primacy
of sex as "binary and biological" (The White House 2025a, The White
House 2025b).

Proponents of these arguments base their critiques on the progressive
view of gender, which they invalidate as a subjective or personal
perception. In the abovementioned Executive Orders, for example,
gender ideology is defined as "an ever-shifting concept of
self-assessed gender identity, permitting the false claim that males
can identify as and thus become women and vice versa, and requiring
all institutions of society to regard this false claim as true." (The
White House 2025a).

Indeed, pro-trans perspectives from medical groups, for example, favor
the notion of gender identity over that of biological sex as a
determinant for how a person identifies. The American Psychiatric
Association defines gender identity as "a person’s inner sense of
being a girl/woman, boy/man, some combination of both, or something
else" ("What is Gender Dysphoria?" 2025). Similarly, the World Health
Organization defines gender identity as "a person’s innate, deeply
felt internal and individual experience of gender," and constrasts it
to biolgoical sex, adding that gender identity "may or may not
correspond to the person’s physiology or designated sex at birth"
("What is Gender Dysphoria?" 2025, "Gender and health" 2025).

Accordingly, some Tran Studies scholars posit the definition of gender
is the problem. That the push to disentangle gender from sex and to
solidify sex as "binary and biological" is a reaction to the fluidity,
nebulousness, and slipperyness of how gender is conceptualized in
trans affirming discourse. Andrea Long Chu (2024), for exaple, points
this out that, "The left must reckon with its part in this. It has
hung trans rights on the thin peg of gender identity". Chu, herself a
trans woman, explains that the pro-trans movement has "largely failed
to form a coherent moral account of /why/ someone’s gender identity
should justify the actual biological interventions that make up
gender-affirming care." In other words, Chu asks, if gender really is
something that is internal, then why should a gender non-conforming or
transgender person need to altar any external characteristics?

The view of gender as an internal phenomenon thus inspires transphobic
reasoning around the issue of medical care, which argues that an
physical transition is not necessary.

This issue is where the paper hopes to make its intervention, by
looking deeply into gender as an embodied and expressive phenomenon,
as theorized by Trans Studies scholars in the theory of "trans
materialism." 

** Gender categories
In what follows, I briefly trace theoretical debates on defining
gender from the perspective of Queer and Trans Studies, with a
particular focus on how it has been conceptualized in relation to
closely associated notions of sex and sexuality.

Contemporary liberal notions of gender descend from a widely accepted
view that gender is a social phenomenon rather than a biological
reality. This view can be traced to Judith Butler's seminal theory of
Gender Performativity, which dissolves the traditionally strict and
causal relation between sex, gender, and sexuality. Butler's theory of
gender performativity stipulates that gender is not, as assumed by the
traditional view, based on a biological reality, nor does it determine
sexuality (with male gendered people prefering female gendered
people). Rather, Butler argues that gender is an effect produced
social norms and expectations which manifests as a series of
behaviors, what they describe as "performativity". According to
Butler's theory, gender becomes real not in any essential or
ontological sense, but through its active and external expression
(1993).

In the 35 years since Butler's theory, the distinction between gender
as a social operation and sex as physical embodiment has been
validated in biology, neuroscience, and psychology (Ainsworth, 2015;
Hyde et al., 2019; Joel, 2021). In other areas, however, the
definition of gender has continued to evolve into the notion of
"identity". Moving from a kind of behavior (one that, Butler is
careful to point out, is not at will, but deeply compelled by social
rules), gender takes on an internal apect of identity or
identification. Gender becomes gender identity, which describes an
internal feeling or identification as male, female, a combination of
the two, or nonbinary genders. Gender as identity thus diverges from
gender as expression, which denotes the masculine, feminine, or
androgynous traits that are visibly apparent and enacted. Furthermore,
in this conception, sex is still understood to be a bodily component,
though one not based firmly in a binary, nor does it necessarily
prioritize any biological marker, like the presence of external
genitals, sex hormones or chromosomes.

Trans Studies scholar Kadji Amin (2022) refers to this splitting of
gender into gender identity a "divergence model" which has
characterized the LGBTQ+ movement in its campaigns for rights and
recognition over the past several decades. The splits in the
understanding of gender has, according to Amin, created a domino
effect where gender is increasingly idealized and divorced from bodily
expression.

Amin argues that this "divergence model" between gender and sexuality
has been responsible for a series of splits between categories like
heterosexual and homosexual, and cisgender and transgender (2022).
According to Amin, the concept of homosexuality used to incorporate
gender-variant forms of sexuality, for example, forms of male
femininity such as the "queen", and of female masculinity in the form
of the "butch", the emergence of transgender offered a new category
that was then used to hold "all manner of gender-bending" [Amin 2022].
 
This view generally accords with modern historiographers of sexuality
who, tracing the categories further back into the 19th century,
explain that heterosexuality as a category is a response to the
creation of the category of homosexual [Foucault 1978, Halperin 2002]:
According to this view, heterosexuality arose as a reaction to work by
19th century sexologists, such as that by Richard von Krafft-Ebing
(1893) and Havelock Ellis (1900), who defined homosexuality primarily
as a pathology. 

Just as heterosexuality emerged "belatedly, as a normative ballast
against homosexuality," Amin argues, so did the concept of cisgender,
which responds to the emergence of the term "trangender" in the 1990s
[Amin 2022]. One particular effect of the emergence of transgender is
to create an idealized notion normativity that do not accurately
describe any gender or sexuality in lived reality, according to Amin.
As a result, Amin argues, terms like heterosexual, cisgender, and
binary represent take up "increasingly idealized and uninhabitable
normative categories" [Amin 2022].

This lack of identificatory feeling perhaps explains why some
cisgender people are apparently reluctant to identify with the
"cisgender" and "binary" nominatives. This is in direct contrast to
those who identify as transgender, who draw from the experience of
discomfort in one's embodied gender, commonly expressed in the concept
of gender dysphoria. It is less straightforward to name and describe
the experience of what is routine and unremarkable as the experience
of being comfortable in one's gender and/or sexuality.

The effect of such idealized conceptualizations of gender and/or
sexuality is to re-stigmatize non-normative gender expressions. Due to
the continual splits within the divergence model, Amin argues, gender
identity becomes buried "deeper and deeper within the private recesses
of the self, where it increasingly disavows any relation to the
social" [Amin 116]. In other words, idealized categories like
cisgender and nonbinary make non-normative gender identities less and
less visible. The effect of prioritizing non-normative gender as an
/identity/ that is invisible, that is not immediately apparent despite
being marked, is to marginalize gender as /expression/, which
re-stigmatizes those gender groups that already experience social
stigma. Amin gives a common example from homosexual culture, where gay
men who are normatively masculine, "masc" or "butch" men, have higher
social status than feminized gay men, "queens" and "fairies", due to
the cultural capital of masculinity (2022). 

There is a tendency to create categories that separate internal
qualities, which are often idealized, from the noticably different
ones. And the reference to "noticably" is crucial here: that it is a
difference which is visually apprehended, perceived to be alternative
from the norm. This norm, by contrast, is transported into an internal
and therefore idealized form, and retains the privilege of being
unnoticed as such.

There is, in short, a kind of stigmatization that accompanies
non-normative genders that are externally expressed. Which is to say
that defining gender as an external expression or embodiment is more
likely to make visible and validate those gender which experience
stigamtization based precisely on how those genders present outwardly.

** Trans Bias in NLP
*** Existing Frameworks for Social Bias
Gender bias in NLP has received much critical attention over the years
(Devinney et al 2022; Blodgett et al. 2021). However, this work tends
to characterize bias through a variety of aspects-—such as stigma,
unequal distribution of resources, and linguistic or syntactic
patterns-—which do not easily fit into a single, unified framework.
Despite this diversity, most studies distinguish between two broad
forms of bias: those based on how bias manifests linguistically
(including grammatical and structural forms), and those based on the
societal consequences it produces (such as representational or
allocative impacts).

Focusing on linguistic manifestations, Hitti et al. (2019) further
separate bias into structural and contextual categories (see Table 1).
Structural bias arises from grammatical conventions—for example, the
use of masculine pronouns as default ("A programmer must always carry
his laptop with him"), whereas contextual bias stems from socially
embedded assumptions and stereotypes (e.g., "Senators need their wives
to support them throughout their campaign"). Shifting attention from
linguistic form to social consequence, Nemani et al. (2023) classify
bias according to the specific effects it has on particular groups,
organizing it into three types: denigration, stereotyping, and
underrepresentation. Denigration involves explicitly derogatory
language such as slurs; stereotyping refers to generalized assumptions
about a group; and underrepresentation denotes the lack or scarcity of
information about certain groups. Along similar lines, Blodgett et al.
(2020) and Barocas et al. (2017) distinguish between allocative
harms—-where access to resources or opportunities is unequally
distributed—-and representational harms, which involve the
marginalization or stereotypical portrayal of groups.

