*** Framework for Trans Bias #1: TIDEs
The first Trans Bias detection method is a project called *Transphobia
Identification in Digital Environments*, or TIDEs (Lameiro et al.
2025). It builds on from the framework of a "trans technology," define
by Haimson (2025) (who is an author of the paper) as a "technology
that addresses the unique needs and challenges faced by trans people
and communities" (Haimson 2025). Trans technology makes trans people a
central focus of a particular technology or tool, in this case, of
detection of transphobic speech.

To that end, TIDEs has two explicit goals: first, to create a dataset
representing varied forms of transphobia, in particular forms that are
more difficult to detect, like TERFism. Referring to "Trans
Exclusionary Radical Feminism," TERF indicates a feminist position
that does not support the rights of trans people. This type of
transphobia is difficult to detect because TERF generally identify as
feminists, although they do not support the rights of trans women and
girls.

The second goal is to center trans and nonbinary experience and
expertise in the data labelling work, which involved labelling social
media posts as "transphobic or "not transphobic". The project chose
labellers who personally identify as trans or nonbinary, and who "have
personal experience with encountering and detecting online
transphobia" (2025). According to the authors, this approach enacts
what they call "care-centered data practices" (2025).

Indeed, the data labelling work showcases the attention given to
individual kinds of transphobia. The dataset comprises social media
posts scraped from Tumblr, Truth Social, YouTube, and Reddit that are
labelled with "transphobic" and "not transphobic" labels. For example,
a post like, "Transgender females are actually biological males. They
should not be allowed to compete in womens sports. Period!" are
labelled as transphobic, while posts like "Transgender Swimmer Lia
Thomas Booted From Olympics After Losing Legal Case" are labelled as
not transphobic.

However, as the project developers point out, some examples are not so
straightforward. For example, the following post
#+begin_quote
Hey guys, I just wanted to get your opinion on this:

Whenever I meet someone I really quickly try to establish whether or
not they have a dick, I don't just restrict this to potential sexual
partners I mean everyone I meet. It just seems super important you
know? Like how am I supposed to sleep at night unless I catalogue
everyone's genitalia?

[...] I've started flat out asking everyone I meet. I try to be polite,
like I look at the ground and draw it out all "Sooo do you like....
Have a dick?" but here is the thing: people look at me like I'm crazy
for asking! What the hell is up with that? I even had one girl tell me
I was a totally creep!
#+end_quote
Detecting this post correctly as "not transphobic" relies on
recognizing its irony, which is present in the tone of enforced
sincerity. In fact, the only substantial indication that this is
written in irony is the end of the post, which asserts that "it's nice
to vent", and the source of the post itself, comes from a
trans-dominated subreddit that emphasizes parody, "r/transcirclejerk".

Because this text, on the surface, parrots a transphobic perspective,
it could be rightly labelled as such. The only thing that makes it not
transphobic is the rhetorical context, whose cues are so subtle that
they could be easily missed, particularly by those who lack the
in-group cultural knowledge and language cues.

Complicating matters further, many other posts use a sincere tone to
make serious requests. For example, the following post that asks for
advice on gender presentation:
#+begin_quote
Hey! I'm going through some gender experimentation, so I'd like to tap
into masculinity for awhile(usually I identify as just pure
androgynous, but I struggle with pulling that off). So, to begin with,
I'll try to master visual/behavioral androgyny first. I have long hair
but don't want to cut it(a lot of manly men have long hair anyway), so
how could I fix it up to look more masculine? Also, how could I dress
more masculine-like? I don't have a very manly looking body shape, but
I'd like to dress to give the illusion of one. Basically, any and all
advice on how to appear androgynous/masculine is appreciated.
#+end_quote
This post, which is rightly labelled as "not transphobic," has more in
common with the previous "transphobic" example in terms of tone (which
is sincere and supplicating. The differences have to do with an
external rhetorical situation that is not clearly visible in the
actual post.

In other words, context matters whether something is transphobic, and
we ought to develop more labels for recognizing context.

*** Framework for Trans Bias #2: EQUITABL
EQUITABL, the *Explore, Query, & Understand Implicit Textual Bias in
Language data* project (Devinney et al. 2024) uses a mixed-methods
approach to analyze social bias in language, which deliberately
includes bias toward trans and nonbinary identities, to examine
"stereotypes, hegemonic norms, [and] erasure of identity" across all
identity groups (Devinney et al. 2024). The target is therefore
broader than TIDEs, which focuses specifically on trans

Another central difference from the TIDEs is that this method
emphasizes a qualitative approach, which combines text generation,
topic modelling, and close reading methodologies. Built from a method
the researchers developed in a previous paper which proposes a
"combination of quantiative and qualitative methods, where the
quantitative part produces a view of the data of a size suitable for
qualitative analysis" (Devinney et al. 2020). For the this more recent
version of the project, the researchers exapanded their methodology to
include text generation, with the main analysis focusing on close
reading a set of LLM-generated narratives.

In brief, the process involves prompting both English and Swedish
language models, Llama 2 and GPT-SW3 using a set of templates designed
to generate narratives about various identity groups, then feeding the
results to a topic model, which will organize the narratives into
topics. Finally, using the resulting topic models to organize the
narratives, they extract the top narratives for each group and proceed
with close reading analysis, which is the main focus of the
experiment.

The templates for prompting the language models consist of three
components: first, an imperative phrase asking the model to "write a
story about" or "write a description of"; followed by the subject of
the narrative, like "a person," "a woman," "a Black cis person"; and
closing with a predicate, such as "falling in love," or "with tears
running down their face" (Devinney et al. 2024). The templates cycle
cycle through different imperatives, racial and gender identities and
religions, and predicates to create many variations. Possible
variations include, for example "a Black Christian at the doctor's
office," "a trans Muslim with a special skill," "a white nonbinary
person with tears running down their face." The result is to create a
diverse set of stories with protagonists spanning a variety of subject
positions, in both positive, negative, and neutral valences.

The study's central intervention is its close reading methodology,
which it includes in the place of quantitative scoring. As the
researchers explain, they intentionally use qualitative metrics in
order to "encourage users to critically engage wit the topics and
patterns... rather than merely being incentivized to chase 'better'
scores" (Devinney et al. 2024). Rather than a bias score, this metric
contains of a series of questions that the researcher uses to guide
close reading the results of the generated text. Some of the questions
include, "How are people and bodies described?" and "Which stereotypes
are used?" They complete the analysis with one more questions that
probe the representation of gender specifically and whether this
representation is associated with certain themes.

The results of the close reading reflect that narratives centering
feminine identities are mostly linked to unpleasant emotions like
depression and anxiety, a quality that is exacerbated in narratives
about trans women. Narratives about masculine subjects, by contrast,
are mostly positive in tone, associated with happy events like
weddings and parties. Additionally, narratives about nonbinary persons
express overt themes of struggle and the need for community support,
and the researchers note that these struggles tend to end on a
positive, "uplifting" note.

Amid these perhaps unsurprising results, there was an unexpected
pattern of refusal from the model, which declined to discuss certain
topics and identity groups. In the refusals, the models explain that
their programming instructions require them to either stay away from
topics of identity and/or to promote "negative or derogatory language"
(Devinney et al. 2024). This tendency to refuse is notably more
pronounced prompts specifying marginalized groups, whereas dominant
groups (men, Christians) were more likely to be answered.

The only exception to this rule occured when identity markers from
socially dominant groups were combined, such as "white" and
"Christian," which created a higher instance of refusal than similarly
intersectional, though less dominant, identity combinations. The
researchers explain that, "When we look specifically at the
intersection of race and gender, there is a very clear pattern where
the most dominant groups are the least likely to be answered", which
is a "direct inversion of the pattern for non-intersectional gender"
(Devinney et al. 2024). For example, the least refused prompt was the
phrase "a man," while the most refused one was "a white Christian"
(Devinney et al. 2024). 

As the researchers rightly point out, refusing to answer a prompt
about a specific identity group is its own form of discrimination:
"The refusals themselves actually produce risk and harm. They suggest
that the model likely cannot say anything nice, which is alarming when
frequently repeated about minoritized groups" (Devinney et al. 2024).
Such refusals implicitly reinforce that only a select and dominant
subset of identities are fit for discussion, while other, more
marginalized groups are not. 

Which relates to another notable aspect of the results, the "unmarked
majority". The researchers explain that "When not otherwise specified,
‘a person’ is assumed to be a man, as well as likely white, straight,
cisgender, and Christian; additionally [...] being a provider"
(Devinney et al. 2024). In other words, a lack of identity
specification leads the model to assume a dominant identity, a default
white, straight, cisgender, Christian male identity. This aspect of
the results perhaps also explains why the model may refuse to answer
generate narratives of intersectional dominant groups ("a white
Christian"). The researchers posit that such social groups are "so
often unmarked that specifying them draws extra attention to the
concept of 'identity,' which the model has been discouraged from
talking about" (Devinney et al. 2024). Due to the dominance of such
groups, reinforce this aspect of the default majority.

** Discussion
In what follows, I discuss how these two gender bias evaluation
frameworks, TIDEs and EQUITABL, engage with Amin's point about
external gender expression and how it relates to stigma. Amin argues
that, due historical tendency of LGBTQ+-related categories that
distinguish internal and idealized identities from visibly different
ones, a kind of stigmatization accompanies non-normative genders that
are externally expressed. Given this focus, how does gender as
external presentation or behavior operate in the above frameworks for
studying gender bias in NLP?

First, it is important to remember that existing evaluations of LLMs
show that the the most frequent and default subject category is the
straight white male, and that other subject categories, like woman,
black, and homosexual, are less prevalent in both training data and
model outputs (Guo and Caliskan 2021). Additionally, research has also
shown a correlation between the presence and frequency of certain
identities in the training data and their association with positive
and negative terms (Devinney 2024, van Loon 2022): van Loon et al.
(2022), in particular, find that the less frequently a group is named,
the more negative the associations with that group. 

If a group's relatively frequent presence the training data has a
positive effect on their representation, one might reasonably assume
that making marginalized subjects /more frequent/ in the training
data, to be on par with the frequency of representation of dominant
subjects, may mitigate bias about these groups in model outputs.
However, this assumption doesn't take into account that the default
subject is often unmarked. As Devinney et al. (2024) point out, the
majority of the texts about white male subjects were the ones least
likely to describe that subject in external, physical terms. 

That is perhaps because of an inverse relationship here between
physical markers of identity and positions of social dominance, which
Devinney et al.'s (2024) work seems to bear out. Here, less physical
description is itself a marker of social dominance. In those results,
white women are described in physical terms only slightly more than
white men, while marginalized identities are described in more detail
than both groups. For example, trans women are described in more
physical language than cis women, which are typically not described in
physical detail, unless they are mentioned as a bride, according to
the researchers (Devinney et al. 2024). In other words, the more
physicalized a person, the more likely that person is from a
marginalized group.

One solution, then, may be to increase physical descriptions of all
subjects--though especially white, male, heterosexual, Christian
identified subjects. Some work already exists toward this end, by Reif
and Schwartz (2023). In their article, "Fighting Bias with Bias," the
researchers share a promising approach: /amplifying/ rather than
reducing bias in a model's training dataset. As they point out,
"filtering [out bias] can obscure the true capabilities of models to
overcome biases, which might never be removed in full from the
dataset" [Reif and Schwartz 2023]. Instead of an approach that reduces
bias, they offer one of increasing it, such as by including phrases
like "the pretty doctor" so that a model will interpret "doctor" to be
female.

Toward this end, researchers might spend more time with close reading
methods, such as those displayed by TIDEs and EQUITABL methods. Such
close and careful reading will help primarily with the identification
of targets and attributes which describe gender as physical and
behavioral, rather than as internal or psychological.

And such methods can apply to multiple approaches to gender bias
evaluation. The TIDEs researchers, for example, developed a robust
qualitative coding system, to make sure they could catch all examples
of transphobia. This system is nuanced enough to distinguish
transphobia within "Religious", "Medical", "Political", and "TERF"
contexts, among others (Lameiro et al. 2025). Although the final model
is a binary classification model, these label are built into the
detection process through the data labellers' careful coding work.

Amin's point--that if gender were conceptualized as an outward,
expressive phenomenon, rather than an identity category, there would
be less social stigma--thus applies to widely different methods for
evaluating gender and specifically trans bias in NLP. Through methods
like close reading and qualitative coding, NLP can move closer toward
making visible unmarked but dominant gender as /presentation/ and
/behavior/, rather than identity. For, as Amin points out,
"Identification is the psychic process that makes the interval between
the individual and the social apparent; it is not the site of their
suture" (2022).

** Conclusion
The problem with the univeral, unmarked subject is precisely that,
despite not being marked, he is overrepresented. The
over-representation of straight white men is not due to a persistant
label of "heterosexual white man" that appends each and every
predication. Rather, it's due to a proliferation of predication that
already surround him, that define him. The white man does not a
descriptive label as such, he simply is.

